Subject: [PATCH] [mlir][bufferization] Compute buffer types from an explicit stack

`bufferization::getBufferType` asks the value's owner for its buffer
type, and an implementation of `BufferizableOpInterface::getBufferType`
calls `bufferization::getBufferType` for each value its own buffer type
depends on (an operand it aliases, a yielded value, a loop's init_arg),
so computing the buffer type of the last value of a chain of N tensor
ops takes N native frames of each, and a long enough chain overflows
the stack of the thread bufferizing it.

The computation now keeps its place on an explicit stack, one frame per
value whose buffer type is in progress: the values of the invocation
stack. An implementation that calls `bufferization::getBufferType` with
the invocation stack it was given gets its next answer, if it asked for
it in an earlier run, and otherwise a failure, with the value recorded;
that run's result is discarded, the value's buffer type is computed by
a frame of its own, and the implementation runs again from the start.
So every implementation is asked for the same values, in the same
order, with the same invocation stack and the same answers as before.
The running computation lives in `BufferizationState`, as the symbol
table cache does. An implementation must ask for the same values when
run again and act on a failed question only by failing, which every one
in tree does; the interface's documentation says so.

Unit test: the buffer type of the last of 10,000 values, alternately a
tensor.cast and an arith.select, is computed on a 256 KiB thread.
---
